FujiNet Config (config-nio) for the Amiga
=========================================

FujiNet Config is a Workbench program for FujiNet NIO. You choose disk
images on your FujiNet hosts and mount them as Amiga drives (DN0: to
DN7:). It runs on Workbench 1.3 and later. The Help menu in the program
explains every page and message.


What you need
-------------

- A FujiNet connected to the Amiga, with the FujiNet NIO drivers.
- The NIO package: the drawer or disk that contains this ReadMe, usually
  shown as NIO:.


Installing on Workbench 2.0 and later
-------------------------------------

Open a Shell and type:

    Execute NIO:Install-config-nio

The installer:

1. Copies FujiNet Config to SYS:Tools (config-nio and its icon). An
   existing icon is kept so your ToolTypes are not lost.
2. Installs FMOUNT, FUMOUNT, FMOUNTRESTORE and fujinet-load-resident in
   SYS:C, and fujinet-nio.device and fujinet-disk.device in DEVS:, if
   they are not there already.
3. Adds these lines to S:User-Startup, if they are missing. The first two
   load both devices at every boot. The third mounts your drives again in
   the background, so a FujiNet that is switched off cannot hold up the
   boot:

       C:fujinet-load-resident DEVS:fujinet-nio.device fujinet-nio.device
       C:fujinet-load-resident DEVS:fujinet-disk.device fujinet-disk.device
       Run >NIL: C:fmountrestore

4. Asks whether FujiNet Config should start automatically with
   Workbench.

Reboot afterwards if the installer asks you to.

To answer the question in advance:

    Execute NIO:Install-config-nio AUTO      install and start automatically
    Execute NIO:Install-config-nio NOAUTO    install only

If the package is somewhere other than NIO:, add FROM= with a name ending
in a colon or slash:

    Execute Work:FujiNet/Install-config-nio FROM=Work:FujiNet/


Starting automatically with Workbench
-------------------------------------

Workbench starts every program in SYS:WBStartup once the desktop is
open. With autostart on, the installer puts a FujiNet-Config icon there.
The icon runs SYS:Tools/config-nio and has the DONOTWAIT ToolType, so
Workbench carries on while FujiNet Config is open.

To stop it starting automatically:

    Execute NIO:Install-config-nio REMOVE

or drag the FujiNet-Config icon out of SYS:WBStartup.


Installing on Workbench 1.3
---------------------------

Workbench 1.3 has no SYS:WBStartup drawer, and its FujiNet drivers are
installed differently.

1. Install the FujiNet NIO drivers and commands with the WB1.3 package's
   own installer:

       Execute NIO:Install-FujiNet-WB13

2. Copy FujiNet Config somewhere on your system disk, for example:

       Copy NIO:config-nio SYS:System/
       Copy NIO:config-nio.info SYS:System/

3. To start it automatically, edit S:Startup-Sequence (for example with
   Ed) and add this line directly after the line that says LoadWB:

       Run >NIL: SYS:System/config-nio

   Save the file and reboot.

On Workbench 1.3 the drives are DN0: and DN1: (double density, FFS),
HN0: and HN1: (high density, FFS), DO0: and DO1: (double density, OFS)
and HO0: and HO1: (high density, OFS).


Options
-------

Set these as ToolTypes (select the icon, then Information in the Icons
menu) or give them in a Shell:

    FMOUNT=path    mount command (default fmount, from the command path)
    FUMOUNT=path   eject command (default fumount, from the command path)
    SCRIPT=file    run commands from a file instead of the window
    RESULT=file    where SCRIPT writes its results

Unknown ToolTypes, and any in brackets, are ignored.


If something goes wrong
-----------------------

The status line at the bottom of the window shows each command's own
message. Help > Troubleshooting in the program lists the common ones and
what to do about each.
